# Benchmarks

Correctness comes first. A performance claim comes with a measurement in the same PR.

## Tools

- Time: `criterion` in `benches/`, run by `just bench`. CI does not run it (noise). It is not a gate.
- Allocation: `tests/alloc.rs` with a counting allocator asserts zero allocations on the evaluation APIs (`.cursor/rules/perf.mdc`). It runs in `just test`. An allowed count stays or goes down; raising it needs an Issue.
- Memory: peak allocation is measured with a counting allocator, build and evaluate separately. The O(N²) RBF cost is measured, not only stated. Against other libraries, memory is the resident set size of the process sampled over time, with the phases marked on it; a peak alone does not say when the memory was held, and an allocator inside one library cannot be laid against another.
- Comparison: `compare/perf/` runs SciPy 1.18 and the other pure-Rust interpolation crates on the same data and queries. It is manual and records results in `compare/perf/out/` (ignored); `docs/performance.md` is the summary, written by `compare/perf/report.py` and not by hand. Procedure: `.agents/skills/perf-compare/SKILL.md`.
- `docs/performance.md` is made from one run of the whole comparison, at one commit, with nothing uncommitted. Its heading (commit, command, "with uncommitted changes") is that of the last run of `run.py`, whatever that run measured: a column or a case measured alone afterwards leaves a heading that is false for every other column. A PR that adds a column or a case commits first and then runs the whole comparison (#151 put this right once; #153 made it again by measuring the parallel column alone).
- A figure that is the same for every case is looked at before it is published. SciPy's memory read 4.3 MB on every row of the report from the first run on (#120): the driver sampled the launcher the `uv` environment starts Python through, and the interpreter was its child. Seven rows of the roadmap regenerated the table; a grid of 200 MB beside "4.3" stopped nobody (#166).
- A run that fails is run alone and read before its row goes into the report. "exit code 101" stood for eight runs since #120; one library's four were a panic of this repository's own runner (#165). A failure is the library's only once that has been seen, and seen means the caller in a backtrace, not the place of the panic: the four of `scirs2-interpolate` panicked inside `ndarray` and were written down as the library's, and the call came from the runner. Before a comparison whose results are published, the driver itself is checked (`just compare-check`).
- A library is not left out for being slow, and the time limit is not set to where one library falls: how long it takes is a figure of the comparison. What is cut is the repeating: a call of two seconds or more is timed once, and such a run is not repeated in later rounds, whatever the library (#160).
- Real-world data is taken whole, in the unit its source gives it out in. What is taken and how it is divided is written in the design before anything is measured, and is not changed after a result (#160: a size chosen by us would look chosen for the result, and might be).
- A call shorter than the step of the clock (100 ns on Windows) is not timed alone: it is repeated inside one sample until the sample is long enough, and the sample is divided. The first run of the comparison timed single calls and reported "200 ns against 100 ns".

## Rules

- The comparison comes before the features that are put on top of the types (`rayon`, `serde`). Where interpx is slower than a library that computes the same thing, that is a row with an Issue, worked before those features, the largest ratio first; or it is written in the design with the numbers and the reason it stays.
- No speed target before the comparison is measured. The other crates are chosen when the row starts: maintained, pure Rust. A category with no comparable crate says so in the table.
- Whether a crate computes the same thing is read from what it makes public: its `lib.rs` (`pub mod`, `pub use`) and every module named there, not from the first module a search finds. In M4-1 `scirs2-interpolate` was left out of the comparison of `LinearNDInterpolator` from its `scattered_2d`, which is not public; its public `triangulation_interp::make_linear_triangulation` computes the same interpolant, to 1e-15 of SciPy's.
- Profile before a speed row, and write on the Issue where the time goes. Order rows by the profile, not by roadmap number. Skill: `.agents/skills/cpu-profile/SKILL.md`.
- Compare both sides with the same profile and flags, the same data, the same queries, pinned to one core when timing a serial path, and interleaved. Stop leftover processes from earlier runs. A difference inside the spread is not a result; "spread" means the statistics of repeated runs.
- A speed PR reports wall time for the inputs the Issue names, next to the Issue's baseline, and measures paths it did not change. A change to code that several types share (the search, the checks) is measured on every type that uses it before any result is reported, on inputs unlike the ones it was written for: the guess of #123 made evenly spaced data 3.6 times as fast and was reported as done, and the grids, whose axes were not evenly spaced, were up to twice as slow.
- A figure that moved is explained from the rows, not from a guess. "The spread between runs" is a claim like any other: look at the cases and the rounds first. In #123 a rise from one case to eight was put down to spread; four of the cases were one type at 1.9x to 2.0x in all three rounds (#131).
- A gain inside the spread is reverted, not shipped with other changes.
- On this machine one criterion run of a benchmark moves by a factor of up to two from one run to the next (a 14 µs benchmark has read 24 µs). A before-and-after claim therefore comes from two source trees built apart and run in turn, three rounds or more, with the best of each side compared. A number that disagrees with its neighbors is measured again alone before it is written down or explained.
- To find where the time of a path goes, cut it short: skip a stage, time what is left, and take the difference. Do this before tuning and before the PR is opened (`.cursor/rules/open-ends.mdc`). The stage that stands out is the one to work on; do not start from the stage that looks expensive.
- A change tried for speed and not kept is reported with its measurement, so that it is not tried again.
- A design that names a way of working for its speed ("the batch uses a hint") is measured before the design PR says so. A prototype in a scratch file is enough. In M2-1 the design said the batch used its hints, and the measurement in the feature PR showed that for points in no order the hints were slower.
- Named baselines: one per milestone (`m1`, `m2`, ...), saved with `--save-baseline`. A hot-path PR compares against the latest and pastes the comparison under `## Verification`. The log is local (`.dev/bench-log.md`, not tracked).
- Do not add SIMD, `rayon`, caches, or mixed precision for speed unless a measurement shows the path is the bottleneck.
- A measurement quoted in an ADR, a comment, or a PR is recorded once, and quoted from there.
- A threshold the measurement applies in two places is applied by the same quantity in both. The runner timed a call once when its first call took two seconds, and the driver ran the later rounds when the median of the samples was under two seconds: a call near two seconds whose samples read slow in one round lost the round after, and SciPy's time on that case read 43% higher (#171).
- A comparison that enters or leaves the losses between two runs has its median compared across the runs before it is put down to the threshold. `CubicSpline` of 1000 points was 2.1 to 2.2 times `ndarray-interp` in the median of every run, and was counted in one and not another by the fastest repeat: the construction takes one of two times by process (P-18, #172).
- A figure in a reply to the owner is computed from the rows, as one in a PR is. A range given from memory in a reply ("12 to 530 times") was 45 to 720 when computed (#173).
- A figure in a document names the library it was measured with. In a table about this crate, a figure of SciPy's is in its own column or says so: the rustdoc of `reciprocal_condition` (#170) gave SciPy's misses and errors as this crate's, and this crate's were three to ten times larger.
- A slow state found in a scratch loop is reproduced in the runner whose figure it explains before code is changed for it. P-18 (#172) built a working space kept by the thread for `CubicSpline`, because a loop in one process lost its slow state with it; the comparison's runner stayed slow, and the cause was the runner timing the 50 to 450 ms after it reads its case, which a warm-up removed with the prototype or without it.
- A length or limit chosen from measurements (a warm-up, a time limit, a tolerance) is measured on the largest cases first, and the cases it was measured on are named with it. P-18 set the warm-up to 250 ms from three cases whose slow stretch ended within 75 ms; `Linear1D` of 10 000 points took 450 ms, and the whole comparison was run once more at 1 second.
